  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 227 PM Thursday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Near-record heat will continue through Friday.\n\nWhile a front across Virginia has dipped into north-eastern North \nCarolina, temperatures have still climbed into the 80s near the \nVA/NC border, with mid to upper 80s elsewhere across the area. As of \n1pm, all three climate sites are within 5 degrees of their \nrespective daily record highs, and records could be tied or \nsurpassed over the next couple of hours. The front should retreat \nback into Virginia overnight, allowing for uniform low temperatures \nin the low to mid 60s. A strong southwest wind on Friday will result \nin gusts between 20-30 mph, and will give one more chance to reach \nrecord highs between 85 and 90 degrees. While clouds will slowly \nincrease during the afternoon, it appears that any rain should hold \noff until after sunset.  \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A strong cold front will sweep across central NC \nFri evening, with a significant cooldown for the weekend. There is \nstill some potential for patchy frost Sun morning and again Mon \nmorning.\n\nA strong cold frontal passage will bring a drastic pattern-shift \nthis weekend and bring the greatest impact to the sensible weather, \nespecially Fri night into Sat. This front will be driven by a deep \nsurface low < 1000 mb racing across the Northeast Fri evening and \nequally, if not more so, anomalous surface high pressure exceeding \n1035 mb diving into the southern Plains and elongating up into the \nMid-MS Valley Sat afternoon. Hi-res guidance continues to support a \nhigh-PoP/low-QPF signal as anafrontal showers/sprinkles appears \nlikely, especially across the northern Piedmont into the northern \nCoastal Plain where strong FGEN through the lower/mid-lvls overlaps \nintense H5 height falls (> 100m falls in 12hrs) and DPVA. \n\nThis pattern will also support the development of an narrow cold-\nfrontal rainband (NCFR), which could result in a period of enhanced \nwinds and wind gusts along and immediately behind the NCFR with \nrapid pressures rises in its wake. Momentum transfer at the top of \nthe mixed layer in point soundings suggest gusts may reach in excess \nof 40 mph with the NCFR passage.  \n\nNWP guidance continues to flip-flop on whether the basal portion of \nthe strong mid/upper level trough will pinch off and become a \nclosed/cutoff low and its proximity to the Carolina coast. AIGFS \ndeterministic runs line up with the AIFS ensemble solutions and \nfavor a farther south and east development of the closed/cutoff low \nbetween the Southeast coast, Bahamas, and Bermuda (physics-based \nguidance favor its development immediately along the Carolina \ncoast), which would favor less rain chances and cooler overnight and \nearly morning lows Sun through the remainder of the forecast period.",
